How to Deploy Your Python AI Agent and Get a Public URL
You can deploy your Python AI Agent to the cloud and access it from anywhere using a web link.

For your Python AI Agent project with LLM, memory, tools, and Streamlit, 
I recommend starting with Streamlit Community Cloud, which offers free hosting for suitable 
lightweight applications.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Prepare your Python AI Agent project
Organize your existing Python files like this:
AI_AGENT/
│
├── app.py
├── agent.py
├── tools.py
├── memory.py
├── requirements.txt
└── .gitignore


Here, app.py is your Streamlit interface, while agent.py contains your agent's logic.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Create the Streamlit application
In app.py, add the code.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This is a working interface example, but the ask_agent() function is a placeholder. To connect your existing agent, replace it with a call to your real Python agent function.
4. Create requirements.txt
This file tells the cloud server which Python packages to install.

For example:
streamlit
requests
python-dotenv


Add your agent's actual dependencies, such as scikit-learn, langchain, or other packages your code imports. Don't include unrelated packages.

Create a .gitignore file as well:
.env
.venv/
agent-env/
__pycache__/
.streamlit/secrets.toml


Keep API keys, private data, and credentials out of GitHub.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Test your application locally
Open PowerShell inside your project folder.

Install dependencies:
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt


Start your application:
python -m streamlit run app.py


You should see a local URL similar to:

http://localhost:8501

Open it in your browser and test your agent.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. Upload your project to GitHub










Open GitHub

Create an account if you don't already have one, then create a new repository named my-python-ai-agent.


From your project directory, run:
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial AI Agent deployment"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/my-python-ai-agent.git
git push -u origin main


Replace YOUR_USERNAME with your GitHub username. Git must be installed, and your 
GitHub authentication must be configured.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Deploy to Streamlit Community Cloud










Open Streamlit Community Cloud

Sign in with your GitHub account.


Follow these steps:
1. Click Create app.
2. Select the GitHub repository containing your AI Agent.
3. Select the main branch.
4. Set the main file path to app.py.
5. Choose an available app URL.
6. Click Deploy.
Example deployment configuration:
Deploy an app
Example

RepositoryYOUR_USERNAME/my-python-ai-agent


Branchmain


Main file pathapp.py


App URL (optional)my-python-ai-agent


These are sample values, not an actual deployment.
Streamlit will install your dependencies and start your application
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

8. Access your AI Agent using its URL
After successful deployment, Streamlit provides an HTTPS address.

For example:
Example AI Agent public URL

https://my-python-ai-agent.streamlit.app
 Copy example URL
Use your own assigned Streamlit URL after deployment. This example link may not exist.
You can now open the actual URL in Chrome, Edge, or a mobile browser, and share it with 
other users if the app is public.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9. Important: If your AI Agent uses your own locally running LLM
Because your earlier project uses your own Python AI model with memory and tools, 
there is one important difference.

A cloud-hosted Streamlit application cannot automatically access a model running on
 your Windows computer.

You have three options:
AI Agent setup	Recommended deployment
Python agent using a hosted LLM API	Streamlit Community Cloud
Small locally trained ML model (.pkl or .joblib)	Streamlit Cloud, if resources permit
Own large LLM running locally (Ollama, etc.)	Cloud server with sufficient RAM/GPU,
 plus Streamlit frontend

For a trained scikit-learn model, you could load your saved model directly:
import joblibmodel = joblib.load("my_model.joblib")answer = model.predict([question])[0]



Make sure my_model.joblib is available in the deployment environment, and load only model
 files you trust.

If your AI Agent uses a large local LLM, you may need to deploy the model 
separately and configure the agent to use its cloud API. Streamlit's free hosting has
 resource limitations. 
Streamlit Docs+1

10. How to update your application after deployment
After editing your Python files, run:
git add .
git commit -m "Updated AI Agent"
git push origin main


Streamlit Community Cloud normally detects the GitHub changes 
and updates your deployed application.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Open GitHub — Create a new repository.
Enter the repository name: jc_myagent
Select private (provided your code has no secrets).
Do not initialize it with 
a README or .gitignore, because you already have a local Git project.
Click Create repository.

2.  Update the existing remote
Instead of git remote add origin, use:
git remote set-url origin https://github.com/JacinthaChandrikaT/jc_myagent

Verify:
--------
git remote -v

Push your Python AI Agent to GitHub:
-----------------------------------
git add .
git commit -m "Deploy Python AI Agent"
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

